(project pricklypear-one-tree
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: one-tree — ONE BIG TREE as the storage substrate, typed nodes for data modelling")
 (status partial)
 (* agent note (|2026-08-02 status reconcile: tree-model/typed-nodes/projections/non-goals implemented; branching-interaction+migration-plan still partial (shared /data A, full tree-diff merge, phase-3 retire tables).|) *)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section one-tree

  (doc "One tree is the storage truth. Tables become projections. Nodes carry types.")
  (status partial)
  (implements image/lib/tree_store.ml image/lib/store.ml image/lib/crud.ml
              migrations/009_tree.sql scripts/verify_one_tree.sh)
  (verify
   (build "dune build @all")
   (test "image/test/tree_store_test.ml — 7 alcotest cases")
   (smoke "ksh scripts/verify_one_tree.sh (tree_verify + eval smoke on :18080)"))
  (details
   (doc (|
        =========================================================
        THE IDEA
        =========================================================
        Everything in Pricklypear is stored in ONE BIG TREE.
        The tree is the source of truth. Code, types, routes,
        data, docs — all of it lives as nodes in one rooted,
        typed, path-addressed tree. Nopales code IS tree
        content: a function's AST is a subtree.

        Today the image already simulates this tree on top of
        relational tables: branches/function_defs/type_defs/
        view_bindings reconstruct hierarchy via branch_id
        columns, parent pointers, and recursive-CTE override
        walks. Merge and promote are tree-diff algorithms
        written in SQL. The relational layer is scaffolding
        around what is already a tree. This chapter removes
        the scaffolding.

        This is the Smalltalk/GemStone lineage Pricklypear
        comes from: the persistent thing is the object graph,
        and queries are programs over the graph. One unix
        image process serializes structural mutation, so the
        classic one-tree-store objection (concurrent writers)
        mostly evaporates — one writer, and a "transaction"
        is: evaluate, commit the new root.

        WHAT CHANGES:
          - The tree is TRUTH. Rows are DERIVED.
          - Storage addressing is by PATH, not by
            (table, branch_id, name).
          - Merge/promote become honest three-way tree diffs
            instead of row-level branch inheritance.
        WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE:
          - The Nopales prim ABI. get/create/update/delete/
            rows/list-bindings/describe/deftype keep their
            signatures. The prim/effect seam — the core
            performs I/O via Control.perform_prim (OCaml 5
            effects; see runtime-effects-transition) and the
            rim handler answers through the Io.DB port — was
            built for exactly this swap. Nopales code and
            route handlers never see the difference. Lwt
            remains only as the v1 rim backend (Io_lwt); the
            direction is effects everywhere, Lwt is the
            replaceable power company, not the model.
          - Postgres remains the physical substrate (byte
            durability, backups, pg_dump). The tree is a
            Postgres-RESIDENT structure, not a new database.
          - additive-only migrations still govern whatever
            physical tables the tree store itself uses.
          - The effects direction (runtime-effects-transition):
            one-tree changes WHAT the rim reads/writes, not HOW
            the core talks to the rim. Core stays direct-style
            via Control.perform_prim; any Lwt still present is
            vestigial rim plumbing (Io_lwt), to be replaced
            (Eio or other) on the H3 schedule — it is NOT the
            model and should not grow.
   |))
  )
  (subsection tree-model
   (doc "The tree: one root, typed nodes, path addressing")
   (status implemented)
   (implements image/lib/tree_store.ml migrations/009_tree.sql)
   (details
    (doc (|
         ONE TREE, ONE ROOT:

           /
           ├── sys
           │   ├── pkg/<package>/<name>     function AST subtrees
           │   ├── type/<type>              type declarations
           │   └── route/<route>            route → handler bindings
           ├── user/<username>/...          same shape per user
           └── data
               ├── u.<user>.<type>/<id>     per-user data nodes
               └── shared.<type>/<id>       shared/seed content
                                             (see branching-interaction)

         BRANCHES ARE LENSES, NOT COPIES. A branch is a
         name → subtree-root pointer into the one tree
         (system/main, <user>/personal, <user>/dev are views).
         Fork = record a new pointer. Override = pin a subtree.
         Unpinned names resolve through the lens to main.
         This replaces today's "recursive CTE up the parent
         chain" with pointer chasing in the structure the data
         actually has.

         PHYSICAL FORM (v1, Postgres-resident):
           - Tree nodes serialized as JSONB (Nopales ASTs are
             already JSONB today — this formalizes it).
           - Path index: materialized path per node
             (ltree or text path column) so "give me all of
             /data/todo" and "give me subtree /sys/pkg/notes"
             are indexed lookups, not tree walks.
           - Per-subtree hashes (content-addressing later;
             detection + vault integrity now). A corrupt root
             must be detectable, and pp-sync snapshot must
             stay meaningful.

         ADDRESSES ARE STABLE. A node address
         (/user/alice/pkg/notes/doc/get, /data/flash-card/<id>)
         is the durable name other nodes reference. Moves go
         through an explicit refile op so references can be
         rewritten (see notes links-and-roam refile, and
         structural-editing for the shared tree-op protocol
         this plugs into).

         =========================================================
         PINNED (P0) — concrete v1 decisions
         =========================================================
         The tree is realized over TODAY's tables first; each
         existing row maps to exactly ONE typed node. Paths are
         DERIVED from row identity (no behavior change):

           PATH GRAMMAR (branch-aware code/types/routes/packages):
             fn     b.<branch_id>.fn.<name>
             type   b.<branch_id>.type.<name>
             route  b.<branch_id>.route.<route>
             pkg    b.<branch_id>.pkg.<name>
           (* agent note (|pkg added 2026-08-02 for lisp-interpreter.package-surface
              Phase 1 — package registry rows (enabled bit + export list)
              are branch-lensed like fn/type/route so per-user enable/disable
              inherits parent->child via the same override walk.|) *)
           DATA (USER-OWNED — see branching-interaction /data
           DECISION, revised 2026-08-02): data is owned by USERS,
           not branches. A datum's owner is the session user at
           create time:
             datum    data.u.<user>.<type_name>.<id>
             shared   data.shared.<type_name>.<id>
           No branch id in the path, no lens walk, no inheritance —
           a session sees exactly its own data.u.<user>.* plus
           data.shared.*. Branches stay purely a CODE concept
           (branching.borg's "branches are for code, not data"
           stands). Fork/merge/promote never touch datum nodes, so
           there are no data carve-outs in those semantics.
           (Legacy pre-revision paths were data.<type_name>.<id>,
           one shared pile — see the migration note in
           branching-interaction.)
           NOTES HIERARCHY (notes.storage — NOT kind=datum):
             folder   data.note.u.<user>.<folder-segs…>
             note     data.note.u.<user>.….<slug>
             heading  data.note.u.<user>.….<slug>.h.<nid>
             Reverse-mirror ignores non-datum kinds; hierarchical
             notes must never be written as kind=datum. The notes
             garden keyed data by USER (u.<user>) from the start —
             user-owned datum placement generalizes that exact key
             to every flat CRUD type (todo, flash-card, …).
           META (system, branch-free):
             user/cred/session/media  meta.<kind>.<id>

           Branch inheritance is NOT baked into the path. The
           override-walk (recursive CTE up branches.parent_id,
           depth ASC, first-occurrence dedup) is REPLACED at
           read time by: try path under self, then parent, up
           the chain — first hit wins. Rehydrate loads
           ancestors-first (depth DESC) so child pins override
           by re-define. Same semantics, honest representation.

           NODE ENVELOPE (jsonb payload per node):
             { v: 1,
               kind: fn|type|route|datum|user|cred|session|media,
               path: text (materialized),
               type: text (datum: type_name; else node class),
               body: json (fn AST / type fields / route
                     {handler,visibility} / datum fields),
               author: text,
               doc: text|null (fn only),
               hash: hex (sha256 over canonical body) }
           Hash covers ONLY canonical body (not path/author) so
           content-corruption is detectable independent of
           placement. Per-subtree root hashes compose from leaf
           hashes (content-addressing later).

           TIMESTAMPS: created_at and updated_at are ROW COLUMNS
           on tree_nodes (see physical layout), NOT duplicated
           into the payload jsonb. They are the source of truth
           for when a node was inserted and last rewritten; the
           upsert ON CONFLICT path advances updated_at = now()
           and leaves created_at untouched. The tree-prim JSON
           SURFACE (node_to_json — what tree/get, tree/list,
           tree/get-uuid, tree/find-by, tree/list-kind return)
           MUST include created_at and updated_at alongside the
           envelope fields, read from the row columns. A node
           returned by any tree prim without these two keys is a
           defect.

           PHYSICAL LAYOUT (migration 009_tree.sql; additive):
             tree_nodes(
               id uuid PK DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
               path ltree NOT NULL UNIQUE,
               kind text NOT NULL,
               type_name text,                 -- datum only
               payload jsonb NOT NULL,
               hash text NOT NULL,
               author text NOT NULL,
               created_at timestamptz DEFAULT now(),
               updated_at timestamptz DEFAULT now())
             -- GIN on payload; btree (kind,type_name);
             -- ltree GiST on path for subtree queries.
             tree_branches(                    -- lens registry
               branch_id uuid PK REFERENCES branches(id),
               root_path ltree NOT NULL,       -- b.<branch_id>
               parent_id uuid,                 -- kept in v1
               head_hash text)                 -- subtree root hash
           branches table stays in v1 (branch_id uuid + parent
           chain); tree_branches adds lens pointer + head hash.
           /meta and /data are NOT under any b.<id> — they sit
           outside the branch lenses entirely. /meta is system
           bookkeeping. /data is partitioned BY USER, not by branch:
           data.u.<user>.* is that user's rows, data.shared.* is
           shared/seed content. A session sees exactly its own
           u.<user> subtree plus shared — visibility is a path-prefix
           confinement, not a lens walk, and there is no inheritance.
           See branching-interaction for the full rule.

           LENS = branch_id + its parent chain. A read resolves
           a logical name by walking the chain's path prefixes.
           This is the v1 lens; true content-addressed pointer
           moves (fork = new pointer, promote = move main head)
           arrive in P5 once the tree is truth.

         NEXT FREE MIGRATION: 009 (001..008 exist). tree_store
         module: image/lib/tree_store.ml.

         SINGLE WRITER: the one image process serializes
         structural mutation. A commit is "evaluate, publish
         new root." No lock manager, no merge-on-write races
         inside a commit. Concurrency control moves up to
         with-transaction over tree subtrees.
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "addressing: resolve /data/todo/<id> and /sys/pkg/notes/<fn> to nodes via path index")
    (smoke "subtree hash detects corruption of a stored node")))
  (subsection typed-nodes
   (doc "Every node has a type; types are the data-modelling layer")
   (status implemented)
   (implements image/lib/tree_store.ml image/lib/crud.ml image/lib/session.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Every node in the tree carries a TYPE. This realizes
         object-model.type-system (planned) as the tree's
         data-modelling layer — types are how the tree is
         structured, not an annotation on rows.

         KINDS OF TYPED NODE:
           - CODE nodes — type is the AST shape (defn, deftype,
             route). A function IS a subtree whose root is a
             `defn` node.
           - DATA nodes — a product type, declared in Nopales:
               (deftype note        (fields (title :string)
                                     (body :string) (tags :array :string)))
               (deftype flash-card  (fields (front :string)
                                     (back :string) (due :timestamptz)
                                     (deck :ref flash-deck)))
               (deftype flash-deck  (fields (title :string) ...))
             Notes, flash cards, todos, calendar events,
             workouts — every product's data model is a set of
             typed node shapes under /data/<type>/.
           - SCHEMA nodes — the type declarations themselves
             live in the tree under /sys/type/, so a type is
             inspectable data like everything else (the
             Smalltalk metamodel-browser feeling).

         FIELD TYPES: as object-model.type-system —
           :string :int :bool :timestamptz :json
           :ref <type>      → a PATH reference to a typed node
           :array <type>    → homogeneous
         In one-tree, a :ref is literally a tree path, not an
         opaque id — dereferencing is a path lookup, and the
         tree can answer "what points at this node" (backlinks)
         from the path index. This is the query roam wants.

         VALIDATION AT THE BOUNDARY: the prim layer checks a
         node's fields against its type declaration on
         create/update. Nopales stays dynamically typed
         internally (per roerick: the product surface tolerates
         jank); the tree boundary is where shapes are enforced.
         The OCaml outer shell is where correctness is proven
         (formal-verification).

         NO INHERITANCE, NO SUBTYPING — composition via :ref,
         exactly as object-model.type-system already rules and
         storage.no-inheritance already argues. One-tree does
         not re-open that decision; typed nodes are flat shapes
         composed by reference.

         RELATION TO TODAY: data_rows rows ARE these typed data
         nodes, packed as jsonb; type_defs rows ARE the schema
         nodes. One-tree does not invent new data semantics —
         it gives the existing semantics their natural shape
         and makes types load-bearing (validation, backlinks,
         structural ops) instead of documentary.
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "create a flash-card node missing a required field → clean rejection naming the field")
    (smoke ":ref deref resolves via path; backlink query lists nodes referencing a given node")))
  (subsection tree-datum-prims
   (doc "Tree prims are a complete data API: tree/upsert, tree/move, tree/delete operate on datum nodes, not only hierarchical kinds")
   (status planned)
   (implements image/lib/tree_store.ml image/lib/crud.ml image/lib/prims_db.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         DATUM NODES ARE FIRST-CLASS TREE NODES (tree-model,
         typed-nodes). The tree prim family must therefore operate
         on them directly — not only on hierarchical kinds
         (folder|note|heading). Today tree/upsert rejects any
         kind outside the hierarchical set, so datum writes can
         only reach the tree through the create/update CRUD
         prims (which generate the path and id internally via
         tree_datum_path). That makes the tree prims an
         INCOMPLETE data API: a product cannot move, reorder,
         or re-parent a datum node with a tree action. This
         subsection closes that gap. Kanban (notes.borg) is the
         first product consumer; it moves cards between
         columns by tree/upsert of the card's datum node with a
         new status field, and reorders within a column by
         tree/upsert with a new position.

         tree/UPSERT with kind="datum":
           - Accepts a caller-supplied datum path of the form
             data.u.<user>.<type_name>.<id> or
             data.shared.<type_name>.<id>. <id> is a uuid.
           - When the path's id label is a placeholder (empty or
             a sentinel), generate a fresh uuid and build the
             path the way insert_datum does, so a caller can
             create a new datum node by path without
             pre-allocating an id.
           - Runs the SAME typed-boundary validation as the
             create/update CRUD prims (check_type_fields over
             body_json against the declared type_name): a
             missing required field is a clean rejection naming
             the field. Undeclared types accept free-form JSON.
           - Writes the tree_node with kind=datum, type_name
             set, the standard envelope payload, and a body
             hash; ON CONFLICT (path) DO UPDATE the body (and
             hash, author, updated_at) so the same prim
             upserts and updates.
           - The reverse mirror (migration 009 forward trigger)
             keeps the data_rows projection in lockstep, so
             (get 't id), (rows 't), and the /data browser see
             the node identically to one written via create.

         tree/MOVE on a datum path re-homes the datum: rewrites
         the path and payload.path of the node (and, for a
         recursive move, its subtree), and the reverse mirror
         syncs the projection. This is the re-parent / re-board
         operation. Moving a datum to a path under a different
         <type_name> is a type change and MUST re-run
         typed-boundary validation against the new type.

         tree/DELETE on a datum path removes the node (and
         optionally its subtree when recursive); the reverse
         mirror removes the projection row. Already
         kind-agnostic in code — locked here as a datum-capable
         prim, not a hierarchical-only one.

         tree/GET, tree/LIST, tree/FIND-BY, tree/LIST-KIND are
         kind-agnostic readers and already return datum nodes;
         this subsection locks that as the contract (a datum
         node is readable by path, by prefix, by body field,
         and by kind just like any other node).

         RELATION TO THE CRUD PRIMS: create/update/delete
         remain valid — they are the id-generating,
         session-user-path-injecting convenience surface for
         the common "insert a new row of type T" case.
         tree/upsert is the PATH-EXPLICIT variant for when the
         caller owns the path: moves, reorders, re-parents,
         and products that model their data as a shaped tree
         rather than a flat table. The two surfaces write the
         same tree_nodes rows through the same mirror; they
         are interchangeable views of one store.

         COMPOSITION SURFACE (no new prims needed for the
         common data operations):
           - FIELD UPDATE on a datum (change one field, e.g. a
             card's status or position): tree/get the node,
             dict-get its body, dict-set* the changed field(s),
             tree/upsert the new body at the same path. This is
             the same read-modify-write the todo package already
             uses against create/update.
           - ATOMIC MULTI-NODE OP (e.g. reordering N cards in
             a column after a drag): wrap the field updates in
             with-transaction so the reorder is all-or-nothing.
           - RE-PARENT / MOVE TO NEW BOARD: tree/move to change
             the path, or tree/upsert with a changed parent-id
             field when the product models parenthood as a
             field rather than a path (the flat todo model does
             the latter — list_id is a field, not a path
             segment).
         The path helpers node-path, type-fields, and
         backlinks are the datum path-construction and
         reference-query surface; they are already
         kind-agnostic and need no change.
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "tree/upsert a datum node of a declared type with all required fields → node appears at the given path; (get 't id) returns the same fields")
    (smoke "tree/upsert a datum node missing a required field → clean rejection naming the field, identical to create")
    (smoke "tree/upsert an existing datum path with a changed field → body updates in place; (rows 't) reflects the new value")
    (smoke "tree/move a datum node to a new path → (get 't id) at the new path returns it; old path is gone; projection in sync")
    (smoke "tree/delete a datum path → node and projection row both removed")
    (smoke "tree/get, tree/list, tree/find-by, tree/list-kind all return datum nodes, not only hierarchical kinds")
    (smoke "tree/get of any node returns JSON with created_at and updated_at keys; created_at is stable across an upsert rewrite, updated_at advances")))
  (subsection projections
   (doc "SQL over the tree: relational rows are derived, not truth")
   (status implemented)
   (implements migrations/009_tree.sql)
   (details
    (doc (|
         The tree is truth, but ad-hoc relational query is how
         you SEE it. Postgres stored procedures / views /
         materialized projections DERIVE (type_name, id,
         fields) rows from the tree:

           - /data browsing, /types, admin grep-queries
           - reporting, pp-sync export, debugging
           - the existing CRUD prim RESULT SHAPES (rows returns
             (id . fields) pairs) are served from a projection
             even once the store is a tree

         DEPENDENCY DIRECTION FLIPS. Today rows are truth and
         the tree is reconstructed by CTE; after one-tree the
         tree is truth and rows are a cache. Projections are
         REBUILDABLE — drop and regenerate from the tree at
         any time. Nothing authoritative lives only in a
         projection.

         OPTION, NOT GATE: the stored-procedure projection
         layer is how relational tooling keeps working during
         and after the cutover. Nopales product code keeps
         calling the same prims; only the prim layer's backing
         moves from tables to tree + projection.

         INDEXING: projections and the path index are what make
         "all nodes of type X" and "subtree at P" fast. A full
         tree walk is never the query path for product or admin
         surfaces.

         CHILDREN-BY-LEVEL, NOT ANTI-JOIN: "direct children of
         P" is expressed as a LEVEL predicate —
           path <@ P AND nlevel(path) = nlevel(P) + 1
         — never as a correlated anti-join (NOT EXISTS over an
         intermediate node). nlevel equality is a constant-bound
         predicate the ltree GiST path index can drive, so the
         children lookup stays an index scan regardless of how
         many nodes share the prefix; the anti-join form cannot
         use the index for its inner <@ and so degrades to
         O(subtree^2) as a subtree grows. Every read endpoint
         that lists one level of the tree (tree/list depth 1,
         folder expansion, the /notes index) goes through this
         level form. (* agent note (|Design invariant recorded
         2026-08-02 from live measurement at ~3000 notes under
         data.note.u.admin: the NOT EXISTS anti-join in
         Tree_store.list_children took ~4000ms to return 11 root
         children (blocking /notes at ~8.6s and surfacing as
         budget_exceeded on dabar) while the level predicate and
         tree/list-kind took single-digit ms; the 100k-step eval
         budget is left unchanged — it is a tripwire that exposed
         this DB-plan bug, not the bottleneck.|) *)

         OUT OF SCOPE (v1): exposing raw SQL to Nopales
         product code. The projection layer serves the image
         and admin tooling; the Nopales surface stays prims.
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "rows 'todo' returns the same (id . fields) pairs backed by a projection over the tree")
    (smoke "rebuild projection from tree; diff against pre-rebuild state: identical")))
  (subsection branching-interaction
   (doc "Branches as tree lenses; the /data question; merge as tree-diff")
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02 /data DECISION revised to USER-OWNED data (resolves BORGE-1785693032): datum under data.u.<user>.* + data.shared.*, RLS floor keyed on user. An earlier branch-keyed draft (b.<branch>.data.*) was rejected as a leaky abstraction — encoded user ownership in a branch path, then needed fork/merge carve-outs. Three-way tree-diff merge / promote-as-pointer-move still incomplete vs scaffolding promote. Code still on the old shared pile — spec leads code here.|) *)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-14 DATA-BRANCHING BOUNDARY (design discussion, with local-first): single-head, user-keyed data STANDS — but it is a current-shape decision, not a hard no forever. Split MECHANISM from CONCEPT: (1) MECHANISM is SHARED — the 3-way tree-diff merge (subtree/node hashes) is one generic tool for both code lenses AND local-first data sync; do not build two merge algorithms. (2) CONCEPT is NOT branchable — data has no user-facing branch concept (no checkout, no 'dev copy of your todos', no lens-walking data). A device lens snapshot (admin/laptop) is SYN/MERGE-BASE METADATA — a replication convenience — not a sibling world; data is SPATIAL (which copy of one datum), not TEMPORAL (which version of the world). Local-first sync gets its merge base from (branch, generation) snapshot pins + node hashes, NOT from making data semantically branchable. REOPEN TRIGGER (falsifies the boundary): a real feature where a USER forks their OWN data to experiment and later merges it back (e.g. clone my notes, rewrite offline over a week, reintegrate). If that use case becomes real, user-keyed flat data creaks and this boundary gets a plan-phase re-look — with the branch-keyed rejection above as the explicit prior to beat. Near-term evidence single-head is right: local-first's first payloads (transcript appends, memory LWW) are deliberately single-head.|) *)
   (implements image/lib/store.ml image/lib/tree_store.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         BRANCH = POINTER INTO THE ONE TREE. Fork records a
         new lens; override pins a subtree; promote moves main's
         pointer to the lens's subtree. This is a strict
         simplification of today's parent_id + branch_id-row
         model: the semantics are IDENTICAL, the representation
         is honest.

         MERGE / PROMOTE = THREE-WAY TREE DIFF. With ASTs as
         subtrees, merge diffing is structural (per-node), and
         last_synced becomes a root-hash comparison. This
         realizes branching.ast-nodes (planned) and feeds
         branching.change-set and review-merge: a change set IS
         a tree diff.

         THE /data DECISION (REVISED 2026-08-02 — resolves
         BORGE-1785693032). The original question was:
         data_rows has NO branch_id — shared data
         (branching.data). One-tree offered two placements:
           A. SHARED SUBTREE — /data/<type>/<id> lives outside
              any branch, lenses reference it. Closest to
              today's semantics; data never versioned.
           B. VERSIONED DATA — /data lives inside each lens,
              fork versions data too.

         REVISION: the A-vs-B framing conflated two axes.
         The axis the rule was WRITTEN for is BRANCH
         VERSIONING (a fork must not copy or wipe the DB).
         The axis that was MISSING is USER ISOLATION (whose
         rows a session can see). Placement A chose global
         sharing to answer the versioning question, and in
         doing so silently made every user's rows readable
         and writable by every other user (BORGE-1785693032).

         The revision closes the isolation gap on the USER
         axis, directly, without borrowing branch semantics:
           USER-OWNED DATA — a datum is owned by the USER
           whose session created it, keyed by that user:
             per-user  data.u.<user>.<type_name>.<id>
             shared    data.shared.<type_name>.<id>
           A session sees exactly its own data.u.<user>.*
           subtree plus data.shared.*. There is NO branch id
           in the path, NO lens walk, and NO inheritance —
           visibility is a plain path-prefix confinement on
           the owning user. (An earlier draft of this revision
           keyed data by BRANCH — b.<branch_id>.data.* — and
           let lenses inherit a parent's data. That was a bad
           abstraction: it encoded a USER-ownership fact in a
           BRANCH path, then needed carve-outs because fork/
           merge/promote must not copy, diff, or move data.
           User-keyed paths state the rule once, honestly.)

         WHY NOT BRANCH-KEYED: branches are a CODE concept
           (branching.borg: "branches are for code, not
           data"). Scoping data by branch would import fork/
           merge semantics that data explicitly does not have,
           and then require spec text explaining why those
           semantics don't apply. The notes garden — the one
           per-user data shape already in the tree — keys by
           USER (data.note.u.<user>.…), not branch. User-keyed
           data generalizes the pattern the codebase already
           chose.

         WHAT THIS PRESERVES (the load-bearing rules):
           - FORK NEVER TOUCHES DATA. There is nothing to
             carve out: datum paths contain no branch id, so
             fork/merge/promote (operations over b.<id>
             code lenses) structurally cannot copy, diff, or
             move a datum. branching.borg's "data is never
             versioned by branch and never wiped by deploy"
             stands, now by construction rather than exception.
           - data.shared is the home of seed/shared content.
           - The Nopales prim ABI is unchanged: (create …),
             (rows 't), (get 't id) keep signatures; the prim
             layer supplies the session's USER from
             Request_context and confines the query to
             data.u.<user> + data.shared.

         WHAT THIS FIXES (the multi-user story):
           - (rows 'note) in alice's session returns alice's
             notes plus shared — NEVER bob's.
           - The /data browser shows the session's own +
             shared rows, not the global table.
           - Cross-user reads require an explicit copy/move
             into data.shared or a deliberate grants
             mechanism — the right kind of friction.
           - NO inheritance of main data: a fresh user sees
             only data.shared (which an app seeds explicitly
             if it wants starter content). Apps own their
             seeding; the platform does not silently clone
             main's data into a user's view.

         RELATION TO NOTES: the notes garden
         (data.note.u.<user>.…) already keys data by USER.
         User-owned datum placement GENERALIZES that exact
         key to flat CRUD types; the notes hierarchy keeps
         its own path grammar (kind=folder/note/heading, not
         kind=datum) and is unaffected.

         ENFORCEMENT FLOOR (RLS): because Nopales is
         user-programmable, prim code is the primary access
         control but not the only one — a missed WHERE clause
         is a cross-user read. Postgres row-level security on
         tree_nodes is the backstop, and it is SIMPLE because
         the key is the user: one policy restricts kind='datum'
         rows to paths under data.u.<current_user> or
         data.shared, keyed off SET LOCAL pp.user established
         per-transaction by Store.with_transaction. No
         branch-ownership lookup, no inheritance to express.
         RLS gives deny-by-default at the single point all
         reads funnel through; the prim layer's path-prefix
         confinement is the same rule stated in app code.

         DEFERRED (still NOT v1): per-branch data VERSIONING
         (placement B — a fork snapshotting its own divergent
         copy of data) remains out of scope. Data is not
         versioned at all: it is owned by users and shared
         only via data.shared.

         TYPE_DECLS: today per-branch rows, table shape shared
         (branching.schema-authority). Under one-tree, schema
         nodes at /sys/type/ follow CODE branching rules
         (branchable, promoted), while /data instances are
         user-owned. Branch code reading user data against a
         newer/older type decl follows the existing
         additive-only tolerance rule.

         COTENANT ISOLATION: lenses enforce
         multi-user.cotenant-isolation — a session is
         confined to its own data.u.<user> subtree plus
         data.shared for DATA, and to its branch lens for
         CODE. This upgrades the guarantee from
         session+branch scoping of CODE to session+USER
         scoping of CODE AND DATA, expressed as path
         confinement and floored by the RLS policy above.
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "fork = new lens; override on lens A invisible to lens B; promote moves main pointer")
    (smoke "user-owned /data: a note created in alice's session is NOT visible in bob's session; both see data.shared")
    (smoke "no inheritance: a fresh user's (rows 'note) returns only data.shared content, nothing cloned from another user")
    (smoke "RLS floor: SET LOCAL pp.user=bob; direct SQL SELECT of data.u.alice.* returns zero rows")))
  (subsection migration-plan
   (doc "Phased cutover: shadow tree → flip reads → projections derive → retire tables")
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02 confirm: 009_tree + mirrors + tree_store + verify_one_tree.sh (P0–P2 territory). Phase 3 retire scaffolding tables not done; PP_TREE_* knobs remain. Added Phase 2b (data user-scoping backfill keyed off author + RLS on user) per the /data revision.|) *)
   (implements migrations/009_tree.sql image/lib/store.ml image/lib/crud.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         NO BIG BANG. The prim ABI is stable throughout; each
         phase is reversible.

         PHASE 0 — ADDRESSING SCHEME (pure design; this chapter).
           Freeze the path grammar (/sys/…, /user/…, /data/…),
           node envelope (type tag + payload + hash), and the
           lens model. No code.

         PHASE 1 — SHADOW TREE.
           A writer mirrors function_defs/type_defs/
           view_bindings/data_rows into the one tree on every
           commit. Tables remain truth; the tree is a derived
           copy. Proves addressing, path indexing, hashing —
           fully reversible.

         PHASE 2 — FLIP READS.
           The rim's store backend (Io.DB impl; Io_lwt today)
           serves reads from the tree behind the unchanged
           prim ABI; tables become the write-through fallback.
           Diff tree-read vs table-read in CI for a soak
           period.

         PHASE 3 — TREE IS TRUTH, ROWS ARE PROJECTIONS.
           Writes commit to the tree; the relational shape
           becomes a stored-procedure/view projection for /data,
           /types, admin. Retire the scaffolding tables (via
           additive-only migration leaving them inert, per
           house rule — never DROP).

         PHASE 2b — DATA USER-SCOPING (the /data revision;
         resolves BORGE-1785693032). Independent of the
         read-flip, one additive migration:
           - New datum path roots data.u.<user>.<type>.<id>
             (per-user) and data.shared.<type>.<id> (seed).
           - Backfill: every existing data.<type>.<id> node
             moves to data.u.<author>.<type>.<id>, keyed off
             the row's author column (today's pile is
             effectively admin-authored, so most land under
             data.u.admin; content meant as seed moves to
             data.shared explicitly).
           - tree_data_rows view rewritten as a user-confined
             projection: rows where path is under
             data.u.<current_user> or data.shared — no lens
             walk, no UNION over a parent chain.
           - CRUD prims pass the session USER from
             Request_context into the datum path (create) and
             the confinement (rows/get/update/delete/find-text).
           - RLS policy + SET LOCAL pp.user land in
             Store.with_transaction; cross-user access test
             added to verify_one_tree.sh.
           The old flat data_rows relation survives one cycle
           as a compat projection, then goes inert per the
           additive-only rule.

         MIGRATIONS AS TREE TRANSFORMS. Schema change stops
         being DDL and becomes Nopales-level migration
         FUNCTIONS over subtrees (more in-spirit; real work).
         Physical tables the tree store uses still change only
         via additive-only SQL migrations.

         BLAST RADIUS: per-subtree hashes + pp-sync snapshot as
         the vault keep corruption detectable and recoverable;
         a corrupt subtree is rebuildable from snapshot.

         DEPENDS ON / FEEDS:
           - object-model.type-system (planned) — realized here
             as typed-nodes.
           - branching.ast-nodes, change-set, review-merge —
             tree diff is their substrate.
           - structural-editing — one shared tree-op protocol
             over outlines AND the storage tree.
           - notes links-and-roam — backlinks come from :ref
             path references.
           - migrations.additive-only — physical-shape rule
             unchanged.
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "phase 1: every commit appears in shadow tree within the same transaction")
    (smoke "phase 2 soak: zero diffs between tree-read and table-read over CI window")
    (smoke "phase 3: drop-write to scaffolding tables; /data + /types served from projection")))
  (subsection non-goals
   (doc "What one-tree is NOT")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         - NOT a new database. Postgres is A physical substrate
           (the server's); the tree is Postgres-resident JSONB
           server-side. A local lens may use another substrate
           (filesystem) per local-first.borg — the prim ABI is
           substrate-agnostic, and a lens is a materialized subtree,
           not a second store. The tree's truth is logical (paths +
           envelopes + hashes), not physical; Postgres is how the
           server holds it, not what it IS.
         - NOT versioned user data in v1. Data is USER-OWNED
           (data.u.<user>.*) or SHARED (data.shared.*), keyed
           by user, never by branch. There is no branch
           versioning of data at all — fork/merge/promote are
           code-only operations over b.<id> lenses and
           structurally cannot touch a datum path. Per-branch
           data VERSIONING (placement B) is explicitly
           deferred — and would now require a wholly different
           path scheme.
         - NOT a type system for Nopales. Typing lives at the
           tree/storage boundary; the language stays dynamic
           (roerick: product surface tolerates jank; the OCaml
           shell is where proofs live).
         - NOT re-opening inheritance. No INHERITS, no
           subtyping (storage.no-inheritance, object-model
           .type-system stand).
         - NOT a REST/GraphQL surface. The image remains the
           API via prims + eval.
         - NOT raw SQL in product code. Projections serve the
           image and admin tooling only.
   |))
   )
  )
 )
)
